{"translation": "Target defines (without proving) the following proposition: For all types X and Y, every natural number T, and every predicate Q on systems of type S1 X Y, if (1) for every system N : S1 X Y whose effective dependency graph is acyclic (N.D9), Q N does not hold, and (2) there exists at least one system N : S1 X Y such that Q N holds, then for every predicate c4 on functions of type {xs : List X // xs.length ≤ T} → Y, there exists a system N : S1 X Y (which may depend on X, Y, T, Q, and c4) such that Q N is not logically equivalent to c4 applied to N's input-output behavior on sequences of length at most T (that is, ¬ (Q N ↔ c4 (N.d24 T))).\n\nHere, a system N : S1 X Y consists of: a finite index type N.A1 of nodes (which may be empty); for each node n : N.A1, a local state type N.A3 n (not required to be finite); an initial global state N.a4 assigning an element of N.A3 n to each n : N.A1; an update function N.a5 taking a node n, a global state σ : (m : N.A1) → N.A3 m, and an input x : X to a new state in N.A3 n; and a readout function N.a6 mapping any global state to Y. Running N on a finite list xs : List X starts from N.a4 and repeatedly applies the synchronous step σ ↦ (fun n => N.a5 n σ x) from left to right along xs, then applies N.a6 to the resulting global state; N.d24 T is the function restricting this list-to-Y mapping to lists xs with xs.length ≤ T. A directed dependency edge N.D8 m n holds when there exist global states σ, σ' agreeing on all nodes k ≠ m and an input x : X such that N.a5 n σ x ≠ N.a5 n σ' x; acyclicity N.D9 means that for no node n : N.A1 is there a nonempty chain of such edges from n back to n (length ≥ 1, including self-loops). No finiteness or nonemptiness assumptions are placed on X or Y, T may be 0, and no extra properties are required of Q or c4 beyond the two hypotheses on Q."}
```

